# Additivity of ordDiff under scaling over a perfect field

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.Place.ordDiff_smul_of_perfectField`
(`5e0cf34b-828c-5cda-8931-efddc8b3afb5`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `dc2a612f-4101-5609-a795-6f37e6773fdf`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra and $K$ perfect, and let $x \in F$ be an element
such that $F$ is algebraic over the intermediate field $K(x) = K(\{x\})$ generated by $x$. Let $v$
be a place of $F$ over $K$ in the sense of this development: a valuation subring of $F$ containing
the image of $K$, different from all of $F$, and whose underlying ring is a principal ideal ring;
write $\operatorname{ord}_v$ for the associated integer-valued order function, defined as minus the
logarithm of the adic valuation attached to the height-one prime of $v$. Let $g \in F$ be nonzero
and let $\omega \in \Omega_{F/K}$ be a nonzero Kähler differential. The quantity
$\operatorname{ordDiff}_v(\omega)$ is by definition $\operatorname{ord}_v$ of the coefficient
expressing $\omega$ as a multiple of $D_{K/F}(t)$, where $t$ is the chosen element of $F$ with
$\operatorname{ord}_v(t) = 1$ (and the coefficient is taken to be $0$ if no such expression exists).
The assertion is the equality of integers $$\operatorname{ordDiff}_v(g \cdot \omega) =
\operatorname{ord}_v(g) + \operatorname{ordDiff}_v(\omega).$$

This is the expected behaviour of the order of a differential at a place under multiplication by a
function, in the one-variable setting over a perfect base field: it is what makes the divisor of a
nonzero differential well defined up to the divisor of a function. It is used in the computations of
$\operatorname{ordDiff}$ of exact differentials $D(f)$ and of pullbacks of differentials along maps
of function fields.

